text:
Who is it talking to?
Every utterance passes through a gate before the model sees it. A wake word has to start the utterance, so “I bought a new computer” on TV doesn’t count. After the assistant replies, a short window accepts an answer without the wake word, so a plain “yes” can confirm something. Stop phrases work at any time. Everything else is dropped without being logged, and no audio is ever written to disk.
Try it. This is a small JavaScript port of the real gate:

examples = [
  "computer, open YouTube",
  "I bought a new computer last week",
  "hey jarvis, turn the volume down",
  "yes",
  "computer",
  "computer thank you for watching",
  "OK stop everything right now"
]
viewof example = Inputs.select(examples, {label: "Example"})
viewof utterance = Inputs.text({label: "Or type one", value: example, width: 360})
viewof followUp = Inputs.toggle({label: "Just replied", value: false})






























wakeWords = ["computer", "jarvis"]
stopPhrases = ["stop everything", "emergency stop", "kill switch", "abort program", "halt program"]
// Whisper's well-known inventions on silence or noise.
hallucinations = new Set(["", "you", "thank you", "thanks", "thank you very much",
  "thanks for watching", "thank you for watching", "bye", "bye bye", "please subscribe",
  "subscribe", "like and subscribe", "so", "okay", "hmm", "um", "uh"])
normalise = t =&gt; t.toLowerCase().replace(/[^\p{L}\p{N}_\s']/gu, " ").split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).join(" ")
wake = new RegExp(String.raw`^\W*(?:(?:hey|hi|ok|okay)\W+)?(?:${wakeWords.join("|")})\b\W*`, "i")

function decide(text, followUpOpen) {
  const norm = normalise(text)
  const stop = stopPhrases.find(p =&gt; norm.includes(p))
  if (stop) return {kind: "stop", why: `Contains the stop phrase "${stop}". Halts at once, addressed or not; a false stop is harmless.`}
  const m = text.match(wake)
  if (m) {
    const rest = text.slice(m[0].length).trim()
    if (hallucinations.has(normalise(rest)))
      return {kind: "wake", why: "Wake word on its own (or followed by a typical Whisper hallucination). Chimes and waits for the command."}
    return {kind: "command", text: rest, why: "Starts with a wake word. The rest goes to the model."}
  }
  if (followUpOpen && !hallucinations.has(norm))
    return {kind: "command", text: text.trim(), why: "No wake word, but the follow-up window is open, so it's treated as a reply."}
  const midSentence = wakeWords.some(w =&gt; norm.split(" ").includes(w))
  return {kind: "ignore", why: midSentence
    ? "A wake word appears, but not at the start. Dropped and not logged."
    : "Not addressed to the assistant. Dropped and not logged."}
}

decision = decide(utterance, followUp)
html`&lt;div class="gate-result gate-${decision.kind}"&gt;
  &lt;strong&gt;${decision.kind.toUpperCase()}&lt;/strong&gt;${decision.text ? html` → &lt;code&gt;${decision.text}&lt;/code&gt;` : ""}
  &lt;div&gt;${decision.why}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`

Treating the model as untrusted
The model can only call the tools in one file, and every call is checked against its schema before anything runs. On top of that:
Tool results are data, not instructions. Text from web pages, apps, files and the terminal comes back wrapped in a tag the model is told never to take orders from, and the wrapper can’t be closed from inside:
def _untrusted(name: str, result: str) -&gt; str:
    body = result.replace("&lt;", "‹").replace("&gt;", "›")
    return f'&lt;untrusted_tool_result tool="{name}"&gt;\n{body}\n&lt;/untrusted_tool_result&gt;'
After two refusals the turn ends, so the model can’t keep hunting for a workaround. After three minutes without a command the conversation is cleared, so old page text doesn’t linger into a new request.
Confirmation is decided in code. git push, uv add, clicking Send, Post, Buy or Pay, pressing Return in a messaging app and typing into password fields all need a spoken yes. The agent reads the action back, and the code, not the model, checks the next utterance:
pending = self._take_pending()          # expires after 30 seconds
if pending is not None:
    if norm in YES:
        return self._run_confirmed(pending)
    self._log_tool(pending.name, pending.args, "cancelled")
The confirmed flag that unlocks the action isn’t in any tool schema, so the model has no way to set it. Deleting is refused outright, and a yes never unlocks a refused action.
An allowlist for the terminal, because a denylist for a shell is a losing game. Commands run as fixed argument lists, never through a shell, with a 20-second timeout. There’s no rm, mv, cp or sudo, and no pipes, redirects, chaining or substitution. Git is limited to everyday subcommands, without forcing or deleting flags:
for arg in args[1:]:
    flag = arg.split("=", 1)[0]
    if flag in GIT_BANNED_FLAGS:
        raise ActionRefused(f"git {flag} can lose work, so it isn't allowed by voice")
    if re.fullmatch(r"-[A-Za-z]{2,}", arg) and GIT_BANNED_SHORT & set(arg[1:]):
        raise ActionRefused(f"git {arg} includes a forcing or deleting flag")
Clicks are refused when the target’s label sounds destructive (Delete, Bin, Erase, Don’t Save, Forget), and refused outright if the target can’t be inspected. Disk Utility, Keychain Access, Script Editor, Automator and Shortcuts can’t be opened by voice.
Every command heard and every tool call (allowed, refused, confirmed or cancelled) is logged to a file only the user can read. “Stop everything” is matched before the model sees it, and a global hotkey (⌃⌥⌘S) stops it from anywhere.

What made it hard

The patient is the unit, not the eye
Each child contributes two eyes and many visits. Two eyes share genes, age, diet and how reliably the child takes their drops, and ten visits from one child are ten looks at one child, not ten pieces of independent evidence. Treating every row as independent would make the evidence look far stronger than it is.
So every model is a mixed-effects model with visits nested in eyes nested in patients: each patient and each eye gets its own baseline offset, and the model learns how alike they are. It also copes with the irregular, gappy follow-up that real clinics produce.


Two questions hiding in one variable
“Does more dilation go with slower growth?” can mean two different things:

Between eyes: are eyes that dilate a lot in general the ones that grow slowly? This is the question as asked, but it can be confounded: children who dilate more might be on a higher dose, take their drops more reliably, or have lighter irises.
Within an eye: when an eye is more dilated than its own usual, does it grow more slowly in that period? Each eye is compared only with itself, so fixed traits cancel out.

Put raw dilation into a model and you get a blend of the two that answers neither. The fix is to split it: each eye’s average dilation (between) and each visit’s deviation from that average (within).
def split_pupil(fu, col="pupil_change"):
    fu = fu.copy()
    # "A big dilator?" and "more dilated than usual at this visit?"
    fu["pupil_between"] = fu.groupby(EYE)[col].transform("mean")
    fu["pupil_within"] = fu[col] - fu["pupil_between"]
    return fu
Try it below. The sliders set how growth (mm/year) changes per mm of dilation, between eyes and within an eye. Set them however you like, and watch the pooled line (dashed) land somewhere between them, describing neither.

function rng(seed) {
  let a = seed &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
  return () =&gt; {
    a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0;
    let t = Math.imul(a ^ (a &gt;&gt;&gt; 15), 1 | a);
    t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t &gt;&gt;&gt; 7), 61 | t)) ^ t;
    return ((t ^ (t &gt;&gt;&gt; 14)) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0) / 4294967296;
  };
}

function normal(r) {
  return Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(1 - r())) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * r());
}

function ols(xs, ys) {
  const mx = d3.mean(xs), my = d3.mean(ys);
  const slope = d3.sum(xs, (x, i) =&gt; (x - mx) * (ys[i] - my)) / d3.sum(xs, (x) =&gt; (x - mx) ** 2);
  return { slope, intercept: my - slope * mx };
}

primary = "#2a6f97"






























viewof betweenSlope = Inputs.range([-0.2, 0.2], { value: -0.15, step: 0.01, label: "Between eyes" })
viewof withinSlope = Inputs.range([-0.2, 0.2], { value: 0.1, step: 0.01, label: "Within an eye" })
viewof cohortSeed = {
  // A plain button in the site's style; each click draws a new simulated cohort.
  const button = html`&lt;button class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm"&gt;New simulated cohort&lt;/button&gt;`;
  button.value = 1;
  button.onclick = () =&gt; {
    button.value += 1;
    button.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", { bubbles: true }));
  };
  return button;
}























simEyes = {
  const r = rng(cohortSeed * 7919);
  const rows = [];
  for (let e = 0; e &lt; 8; e++) {
    const typical = 0.2 + e / 7 + 0.1 * normal(r);
    const offset = 0.02 * normal(r);
    for (let v = 0; v &lt; 6; v++) {
      rows.push({ eye: `Eye ${e + 1}`, typical, offset, dev: 0.25 * normal(r), noise: 0.02 * normal(r) });
    }
  }
  return rows;
}

explainerPoints = simEyes.map((d) =&gt; ({
  ...d,
  x: d.typical + d.dev,
  y: 0.25 + betweenSlope * (d.typical - 0.7) + withinSlope * d.dev + d.offset + d.noise
}))

explainerFits = {
  const pooled = ols(explainerPoints.map((d) =&gt; d.x), explainerPoints.map((d) =&gt; d.y));
  const eyes = d3.groups(explainerPoints, (d) =&gt; d.eye).map(([eye, ps]) =&gt; ({
    eye, ps, mx: d3.mean(ps, (d) =&gt; d.x), my: d3.mean(ps, (d) =&gt; d.y)
  }));
  const between = ols(eyes.map((g) =&gt; g.mx), eyes.map((g) =&gt; g.my));
  const wx = eyes.flatMap((g) =&gt; g.ps.map((p) =&gt; p.x - g.mx));
  const wy = eyes.flatMap((g) =&gt; g.ps.map((p) =&gt; p.y - g.my));
  return { pooled, between, within: ols(wx, wy).slope, eyes };
}























{
  const [lo, hi] = d3.extent(explainerPoints, (d) =&gt; d.x);
  const line = (f) =&gt; [{ x: lo, y: f.intercept + f.slope * lo }, { x: hi, y: f.intercept + f.slope * hi }];
  const w = explainerFits.within;
  return Plot.plot({
    width,
    height: 380,
    marginLeft: 50,
    x: { label: "Pupil dilation since starting atropine (mm) →" },
    y: { label: "↑ Eye growth (mm/year)" },
    color: { scheme: "tableau10" },
    caption: "Simulated data. Coloured short lines: within each eye. Blue: between eyes. Dashed: everything pooled.",
    marks: [
      Plot.dot(explainerPoints, { x: "x", y: "y", fill: "eye", r: 3.5, fillOpacity: 0.7 }),
      Plot.link(explainerFits.eyes, {
        x1: (g) =&gt; g.mx - 0.3, y1: (g) =&gt; g.my - 0.3 * w,
        x2: (g) =&gt; g.mx + 0.3, y2: (g) =&gt; g.my + 0.3 * w,
        stroke: "eye", strokeWidth: 2
      }),
      Plot.line(line(explainerFits.between), { x: "x", y: "y", stroke: primary, strokeWidth: 3 }),
      Plot.line(line(explainerFits.pooled), { x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "currentColor", strokeDasharray: "6 4", strokeWidth: 1.5 })
    ]
  });
}







md`Fitted slopes, in mm/year per mm of dilation: **between ${explainerFits.between.slope.toFixed(2)}**,
**within ${explainerFits.within.toFixed(2)}**, and **pooled ${explainerFits.pooled.slope.toFixed(2)}**,
which matches neither.`






The question as asked compares children, so the between-eye effect was the primary result and the within-eye effect secondary, with the confounding risk of the between comparison written into the plan’s limitations before anything was fitted.


No untreated group
Every child’s first visit is before atropine and every later visit is on it, so there’s no untreated comparison group. Each eye’s own pre-treatment visit is the only “before”.
To see what an untreated history might add, I ran a side experiment: a synthetic ghost baseline, inventing years of untreated growth before each eye’s first visit at typical untreated rates. It was useful and also showed its own limits. Because the invented growth was unrelated to pupil size, it could confirm the atropine effect I had built into it, but could never reveal a pupil–response link. It’s kept apart from the main analysis and labelled exploratory.


A messy export
The raw IOLMaster export needed real work before any analysis:

a file-version line above the column names;
test records mixed in with patients, and IDs that had to be checked one-to-one with patients;
repeat scans on the same day, some marked “Successful” and some only “Warning”;
eyes that appear to shrink between visits, usually a measurement or left/right label error;
pupil sizes taken under unrecorded lighting, so the exposure itself is noisy.

All of this is handled in code, never by hand in a spreadsheet: suspect rows are flagged, not deleted, so the main analysis keeps them and the sensitivity analyses test what happens without them.

trajectories = {
  const r = rng(42);
  const rows = [];
  for (let e = 0; e &lt; 16; e++) {
    const base = 24 + 0.9 * normal(r);
    const rate = Math.max(0.03, 0.2 + 0.08 * normal(r));
    for (let t = 0; t &lt;= 3.2; t += 0.35 + 0.4 * r()) {
      rows.push({ eye: e, years: t, AL: base + rate * t * (1 - 0.08 * t) + 0.015 * normal(r) });
    }
  }
  return rows;
}

Plot.plot({
  width,
  height: 320,
  marginLeft: 50,
  x: { label: "Years since starting atropine →" },
  y: { label: "↑ Axial length (mm)" },
  caption: "Simulated trajectories, one line per eye, to show the shape of the data: uneven visit timing and different growth rates.",
  marks: [
    Plot.line(trajectories, { x: "years", y: "AL", z: "eye", stroke: primary, strokeOpacity: 0.45 }),
    Plot.dot(trajectories, { x: "years", y: "AL", fill: primary, r: 2, fillOpacity: 0.6 })
  ]
})

What it found
This was set up as an estimation study, not a hunt for p &lt; 0.05: the aim was the size of the association and its confidence interval, read against how fast myopic eyes grow, so the answer is useful whichever way it goes.
Read that way, no clinically meaningful association was detected between how much a child’s pupils dilate and how fast their eyes grow on atropine. That’s a useful answer in itself: pupil dilation alone shouldn’t be read as a sign the treatment is working. Because the pupil measurements are noisy, it also doesn’t rule out a real but small link, which better measurement could test.

forestRows = {
  const r = rng(2026);
  const labels = [
    "Primary model",
    "Pupil at interval start",
    "Cumulative growth",
    "GEE (population average)",
    "Cluster bootstrap",
    "One eye per patient",
    "Other interval rules",
    "Season term",
    "Eyes with 3+ intervals",
    "Drop flagged intervals",
    "Drop Warning-only scans",
    "Child-level dilation",
    "Device software version"
  ];
  return labels.map((label, i) =&gt; {
    const est = 0.006 * normal(r), half = 0.015 + 0.012 * r();
    return { label, est, lo: est - half, hi: est + half, primary: i === 0 };
  });
}

Plot.plot({
  width,
  height: 420,
  marginLeft: 190,
  // Only zero is labelled, so the simulated values can't be read as a real effect size.
  x: { label: "Effect estimate with 95% CI (simulated) →", ticks: [0] },
  y: { label: null, domain: forestRows.map((d) =&gt; d.label) },
  caption: "Illustrative layout with simulated numbers, not study results: one row per analysis, so agreement (or not) is visible at a glance.",
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleX([0], { stroke: "currentColor", strokeOpacity: 0.4 }),
    Plot.link(forestRows, { x1: "lo", x2: "hi", y1: "label", y2: "label", stroke: (d) =&gt; (d.primary ? primary : "currentColor"), strokeWidth: 2 }),
    Plot.dot(forestRows, { x: "est", y: "label", fill: (d) =&gt; (d.primary ? primary : "currentColor"), r: 4 })
  ]
})
